faq:
  - q: "What is a nearshore forward deployed engineer?"
    a: "A nearshore forward deployed engineer (FDE) is a senior engineer from a nearby, time-zone-aligned country who works embedded in the client's team and owns outcomes in production, not just assigned tickets."
  - q: "How is a forward deployed engineer different from staff augmentation?"
    a: "Staff augmentation adds capacity to a backlog. A forward deployed engineer is accountable for what reaches production: they learn the business and its constraints, shape the work with your leads, and build with AI agents inside a reviewed workflow. Our Talent Augmentation contracts are how FDEs usually join a team."
  - q: "Can a forward deployed engineer be fractional?"
    a: "Yes. Senior experts can join for 15 to 20 hours a week as part of an Agentic Engineering engagement, or full time when the work needs a dedicated owner."
  - q: "What time zone do your engineers work in?"
    a: "US hours. Our engineers are in Mexico and Latin America, and Monterrey runs on Central Standard Time year-round, so standups, reviews and incidents happen inside your working day."
  - q: "How fast can a forward deployed engineer start?"
    a: "We present matched candidate profiles within days. You run the final interviews, and the engineer joins your workflows and tools from day one."
  - q: "Who manages a forward deployed engineer day to day?"
    a: "You direct the work alongside your leads. We handle employment, growth plans, performance reviews and continuity behind them, so the knowledge stays with your team."
